1
00:00:00,152 --> 00:00:03,112
Here is a question for a language model. What

2
00:00:03,112 --> 00:00:06,092
is the capital of India? You type it, you

3
00:00:06,092 --> 00:00:08,922
send it, and an answer comes back. It looks

4
00:00:08,922 --> 00:00:11,611
as though the model is reading your words directly.

5
00:00:11,724 --> 00:00:14,664
It is not. Before the model sees anything at

6
00:00:14,664 --> 00:00:17,664
all, that sentence is taken apart and turned into

7
00:00:17,664 --> 00:00:21,974
something else entirely. Watch what the model actually receives.

8
00:00:21,974 --> 00:00:23,411
A list of integers.

9
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:26,442
One number for every piece of the text. No

10
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:32,412
letters, no words, no punctuation, just positions in a

11
00:00:32,412 --> 00:00:34,371
table.

12
00:00:35,375 --> 00:00:38,495
So how does text become numbers? That is what

13
00:00:38,495 --> 00:00:39,898
this episode is about.

14
00:00:40,825 --> 00:00:44,225
Start from the simplest idea. A tokenizer needs a

15
00:00:44,225 --> 00:00:48,203
dictionary. On the left, readable text. On the right,

16
00:00:48,203 --> 00:00:51,245
a list of integers. The dictionary is the bridge

17
00:00:51,245 --> 00:00:52,558
between the two worlds.

18
00:00:52,759 --> 00:00:57,168
Take a short greeting: Hello, with an exclamation mark.

19
00:00:57,168 --> 00:00:59,809
The tokenizer does not keep it whole. It cuts

20
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:03,299
it into two pieces. Hello, and the exclamation mark

21
00:01:03,299 --> 00:01:06,544
on its own. Each piece is called a token.

22
00:01:06,699 --> 00:01:09,732
Every token then looks itself up in the dictionary.

23
00:01:09,732 --> 00:01:13,119
Hello is entry forty two. The exclamation mark is

24
00:01:13,119 --> 00:01:16,139
entry ninety one. Those two integers are what the

25
00:01:16,139 --> 00:01:19,719
model receives. I invented these particular numbers to keep

26
00:01:19,719 --> 00:01:22,719
the picture clean, but the mechanism is exactly what

27
00:01:22,719 --> 00:01:24,504
every real tokenizer does.

28
00:01:25,157 --> 00:01:28,868
For a long time, the dictionary held whole words.

29
00:01:28,868 --> 00:01:31,217
Every word the system knew got its own drawer

30
00:01:31,217 --> 00:01:37,388
in the cabinet. Play. Player. Playing. Played. Four drawers,

31
00:01:37,388 --> 00:01:38,671
four entries, for one idea.

32
00:01:38,838 --> 00:01:41,888
That works beautifully until somebody uses a word that

33
00:01:41,888 --> 00:01:45,368
was never in the training data. Playfulness. There is

34
00:01:45,368 --> 00:01:48,678
no drawer for it. The searchlight sweeps the cabinet

35
00:01:48,678 --> 00:01:51,961
and finds nothing. So the word is thrown away,

36
00:01:51,961 --> 00:01:55,778
and replaced by a single marker: unknown token. The

37
00:01:55,778 --> 00:01:58,828
word is gone. The model cannot tell you what

38
00:01:58,828 --> 00:01:59,638
it was.

39
00:02:00,466 --> 00:02:02,996
The book makes exactly this point with a different

40
00:02:02,996 --> 00:02:09,806
family: apology, apologize, apologetic, apologist. Four whole words, all

41
00:02:09,806 --> 00:02:12,916
built on one stem, and the vocabulary pays for

42
00:02:12,916 --> 00:02:15,596
every one of them separately. That is a lot

43
00:02:15,596 --> 00:02:18,004
of storage for very little difference.

44
00:02:18,092 --> 00:02:20,542
But look closely at those drawers, and a pattern

45
00:02:20,542 --> 00:02:25,732
appears. The same letters keep coming back. Play. Play.

46
00:02:25,732 --> 00:02:29,692
Play. If those letters could be shared, one entry

47
00:02:29,692 --> 00:02:30,531
would do the work of many.

48
00:02:31,139 --> 00:02:35,461
So here is one escape route. Forget words entirely.

49
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:39,259
Treat every single character as a token. Playful shatters

50
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:43,709
into seven separate pieces. P. L. A. Y. F.

51
00:02:43,709 --> 00:02:44,631
U. L.

52
00:02:44,852 --> 00:02:48,672
Now nothing is ever unknown again, because every word

53
00:02:48,672 --> 00:02:51,652
in every language is built from characters the system

54
00:02:51,652 --> 00:02:55,092
already has. But look at the price. The word

55
00:02:55,092 --> 00:02:58,122
play, which used to be one token, is now

56
00:02:58,122 --> 00:02:58,931
four.

57
00:02:59,099 --> 00:03:02,056
And that price is paid in the model's context.

58
00:03:02,056 --> 00:03:05,299
Subword tokens average about three characters each, so a

59
00:03:05,299 --> 00:03:08,219
context of one thousand and twenty four tokens holds

60
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:11,151
roughly three times as much text as characters would.

61
00:03:11,398 --> 00:03:14,748
Character tokenization is not wrong. It is a different

62
00:03:14,748 --> 00:03:18,588
trade: complete coverage, bought with much longer sequences for

63
00:03:18,588 --> 00:03:19,984
the model to work through.

64
00:03:20,519 --> 00:03:23,399
So the machine takes the middle path. Keep whole

65
00:03:23,399 --> 00:03:25,929
words when they are common. Break the rare ones

66
00:03:25,929 --> 00:03:31,009
into reusable pieces. Play, player, playing. All three share

67
00:03:31,009 --> 00:03:32,178
the same opening.

68
00:03:32,404 --> 00:03:36,781
That shared opening collapses into a single block: play.

69
00:03:36,781 --> 00:03:40,454
Now the endings can be stored separately. Er. And

70
00:03:40,454 --> 00:03:44,218
ing. Three words, built from three pieces.

71
00:03:44,853 --> 00:03:48,696
And a word the vocabulary has never seen? Playful.

72
00:03:48,696 --> 00:03:51,223
It needs no new entry at all. Play is

73
00:03:51,223 --> 00:03:54,553
already there. Ful is already there. The word is

74
00:03:54,553 --> 00:03:55,731
simply assembled.

75
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:59,390
That is what the book calls subword tokenization. It

76
00:03:59,390 --> 00:04:03,800
is why the pieces for apology, apologize, apologetic and

77
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,710
apologist can be shared, instead of stored whole. Whole

78
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:10,551
words when useful. Smaller pieces when needed.

79
00:04:11,328 --> 00:04:16,068
Three decisions shape every tokenizer. First, the method: the

80
00:04:16,068 --> 00:04:19,788
algorithm that learns where to cut. Byte pair encoding

81
00:04:19,788 --> 00:04:23,718
for the GPT family. WordPiece for BERT. They aim

82
00:04:23,718 --> 00:04:25,868
at the same goal and reach it in different

83
00:04:25,868 --> 00:04:26,604
ways.

84
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:31,140
Second, the parameters, and the most visible one is

85
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:34,230
vocabulary size. These are the real numbers from the

86
00:04:34,230 --> 00:04:38,220
book. BERT uncased holds thirty thousand five hundred and

87
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:42,690
twenty two entries. GPT-2 holds fifty thousand two hundred

88
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:46,360
and fifty seven. GPT-4 holds a little over one

89
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:47,471
hundred thousand.

90
00:04:47,743 --> 00:04:51,033
Third, the data the tokenizer is trained on. Same

91
00:04:51,033 --> 00:04:54,193
method, same size, but trained on code instead of

92
00:04:54,193 --> 00:04:57,193
prose, and you get a different vocabulary. So a

93
00:04:57,193 --> 00:04:59,593
method name alone can never tell you how a

94
00:04:59,593 --> 00:05:00,917
word will be split.

95
00:05:01,015 --> 00:05:06,875
Every tokenizer also reserves special tokens. Unknown, padding, beginning

96
00:05:06,875 --> 00:05:10,945
and end of text, separator, mask. These are not

97
00:05:10,945 --> 00:05:13,711
words. They are instructions to the model.

98
00:05:14,314 --> 00:05:17,094
Now the real thing. The book runs this next

99
00:05:17,094 --> 00:05:20,634
example on the Phi-3 tokenizer, which reuses the Llama

100
00:05:20,634 --> 00:05:23,894
2 vocabulary and adds chat tokens. Here is our

101
00:05:23,894 --> 00:05:25,611
word: apologizing.

102
00:05:25,874 --> 00:05:28,634
The tokenizer does not keep it whole, and it

103
00:05:28,634 --> 00:05:31,424
does not fall back to letters. It returns two

104
00:05:31,424 --> 00:05:35,934
pieces: apolog, and izing. Neither piece is a word

105
00:05:35,934 --> 00:05:37,011
on its own.

106
00:05:37,754 --> 00:05:40,764
Each piece is an entry in the vocabulary, and

107
00:05:40,764 --> 00:05:44,524
each entry has an integer. Apolog is twenty seven

108
00:05:44,524 --> 00:05:48,204
thousand seven hundred and forty six. Izing is five

109
00:05:48,204 --> 00:05:51,064
thousand two hundred and eighty one. Those are the

110
00:05:51,064 --> 00:05:52,871
numbers the model receives.

111
00:05:53,627 --> 00:05:56,037
And there is a detail you cannot see in

112
00:05:56,037 --> 00:05:59,847
ordinary text. Izing carries a hidden character at its

113
00:05:59,847 --> 00:06:03,561
start, meaning: I am joined to what came before.

114
00:06:03,561 --> 00:06:05,927
A token without that marker is read as starting

115
00:06:05,927 --> 00:06:09,423
a new word. Whitespace is part of the encoding,

116
00:06:09,423 --> 00:06:10,937
not part of the string.

117
00:06:11,661 --> 00:06:15,531
The same machinery runs in reverse. The model's output

118
00:06:15,531 --> 00:06:18,564
is not text either. It is more integers.

119
00:06:18,784 --> 00:06:22,374
Three thousand three hundred and twenty three. Six hundred

120
00:06:22,374 --> 00:06:24,936
and twenty two. Look them up and you get

121
00:06:24,936 --> 00:06:29,464
Sub, and ject. Decoded together, they read Subject.

122
00:06:29,693 --> 00:06:32,323
So the tokenizer stands on both sides of the

123
00:06:32,323 --> 00:06:36,123
model. Text to IDs on the way in. IDs

124
00:06:36,123 --> 00:06:38,723
back to text on the way out. That is

125
00:06:38,723 --> 00:06:42,004
why the same component appears twice in the pipeline.

126
00:06:42,646 --> 00:06:45,626
Time for the experiment. The book takes one single

127
00:06:45,626 --> 00:06:48,446
string and runs it through one trained tokenizer after

128
00:06:48,446 --> 00:06:51,626
another, so the only thing changing is the tokenizer

129
00:06:51,626 --> 00:06:52,311
itself.

130
00:06:52,893 --> 00:06:55,843
Here are four of them. BERT uncased, which uses

131
00:06:55,843 --> 00:07:01,583
WordPiece. GPT-2, which uses byte pair encoding. FLAN-T5, which

132
00:07:01,583 --> 00:07:06,423
uses SentencePiece. And GPT-4, also byte pair encoding, with

133
00:07:06,423 --> 00:07:07,831
a much larger vocabulary.

134
00:07:09,325 --> 00:07:12,805
Notice the distinction. WordPiece and byte pair encoding are

135
00:07:12,805 --> 00:07:16,615
algorithms. SentencePiece is a framework that can implement more

136
00:07:16,615 --> 00:07:20,155
than one of them. And FLAN-T5 and GPT-4 are

137
00:07:20,155 --> 00:07:23,445
models that own a particular trained tokenizer. Keep those

138
00:07:23,445 --> 00:07:26,111
separate, and the rest of this comparison makes sense.

139
00:07:26,359 --> 00:07:29,729
The book compares eight trained tokenizers in total. We

140
00:07:29,729 --> 00:07:32,139
are showing four, because four is enough to see

141
00:07:32,139 --> 00:07:35,151
the disagreements. And they do disagree.

142
00:07:35,865 --> 00:07:39,380
Look at one word from the test string: capitalization,

143
00:07:39,380 --> 00:07:40,924
written in capitals.

144
00:07:41,139 --> 00:07:45,469
BERT uncased lowercases its input first. So the capitals

145
00:07:45,469 --> 00:07:48,629
are gone before anything else happens. It then splits

146
00:07:48,629 --> 00:07:53,178
the word into capital, and hash-hash ization. Two pieces.

147
00:07:53,178 --> 00:07:56,739
Those double hashes mark a continuation: this token is

148
00:07:56,739 --> 00:07:58,351
joined to the one before it.

149
00:07:58,554 --> 00:08:01,634
But BERT also ships in a cased version, and

150
00:08:01,634 --> 00:08:05,198
it does not lowercase. The very same word becomes

151
00:08:05,198 --> 00:08:09,312
eight pieces. Eight. That is four times the cost,

152
00:08:09,312 --> 00:08:10,464
for one word.

153
00:08:11,091 --> 00:08:16,391
GPT-2 keeps the capitals and uses four pieces. FLAN-T5

154
00:08:16,391 --> 00:08:23,211
uses five. GPT-4 uses two: CAPITAL, and IZATION. Same

155
00:08:23,211 --> 00:08:26,504
input, same word, four different answers.

156
00:08:26,807 --> 00:08:29,017
And there is a cost that never shows up

157
00:08:29,017 --> 00:08:32,287
in a token count. BERT uncased throws the newline

158
00:08:32,287 --> 00:08:35,267
characters away completely. Which means a chat log, where

159
00:08:35,267 --> 00:08:37,837
every turn starts on a new line, loses that

160
00:08:37,837 --> 00:08:40,071
structure before the model ever sees it.

161
00:08:40,827 --> 00:08:42,917
Now the part of the test string that breaks

162
00:08:42,917 --> 00:08:46,787
the older tokenizers. A musical note, and the Chinese

163
00:08:46,787 --> 00:08:48,218
character for bird.

164
00:08:48,337 --> 00:08:52,567
BERT replaces both with unknown. FLAN-T5 does the same

165
00:08:52,567 --> 00:08:55,357
thing, using its own marker. And here is why

166
00:08:55,357 --> 00:08:59,116
that matters: the marker records that something was there.

167
00:08:59,116 --> 00:09:01,937
It does not record what. Look the marker up,

168
00:09:01,937 --> 00:09:03,851
and the character is simply gone.

169
00:09:03,973 --> 00:09:06,713
GPT-2 takes a different route. It falls back to

170
00:09:06,713 --> 00:09:09,793
the raw bytes that make up the character. The

171
00:09:09,793 --> 00:09:13,653
musical note is three bytes, and they become three

172
00:09:13,653 --> 00:09:16,773
tokens. Printed on their own they look like replacement

173
00:09:16,773 --> 00:09:18,263
characters, because a single byte is not a readable

174
00:09:18,263 --> 00:09:19,084
character.

175
00:09:19,364 --> 00:09:25,274
Their identifiers are 8582, 236 and 113. Decode them

176
00:09:25,274 --> 00:09:28,064
together, as a group, and the note comes back

177
00:09:28,064 --> 00:09:31,514
intact. The book prints no identifiers for the Chinese

178
00:09:31,514 --> 00:09:33,558
character, so neither do we.

179
00:09:33,748 --> 00:09:37,108
That is the trade. Byte fallback keeps coverage, so

180
00:09:37,108 --> 00:09:40,408
nothing is ever simply lost. But a single character

181
00:09:40,408 --> 00:09:43,058
can cost several tokens, and every one of them

182
00:09:43,058 --> 00:09:45,064
takes a position in the context.

183
00:09:45,768 --> 00:09:50,090
One more kind of input decides the outcome: whitespace.

184
00:09:50,090 --> 00:09:53,168
Here is the book's own code example. Those dots

185
00:09:53,168 --> 00:09:56,652
mark four spaces of indentation. They are not code

186
00:09:56,652 --> 00:09:59,024
— they are showing you what is invisible.

187
00:09:59,134 --> 00:10:02,594
Send those four spaces through a character-level tokenizer and

188
00:10:02,594 --> 00:10:05,534
you get four separate tokens. The model then has

189
00:10:05,534 --> 00:10:07,774
to work out that they belong together, and how

190
00:10:07,774 --> 00:10:09,451
deep the indentation is.

191
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:13,220
GPT-4 does something better here. It holds a single

192
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:16,650
token for four consecutive spaces. In fact it has

193
00:10:16,650 --> 00:10:19,530
a dedicated token for every run of whitespace up

194
00:10:19,530 --> 00:10:22,910
to eighty three. And the Python keyword elif gets

195
00:10:22,910 --> 00:10:24,204
a token of its own.

196
00:10:24,358 --> 00:10:28,488
StarCoder2 is a code specialist, and pushes further. It

197
00:10:28,488 --> 00:10:31,558
gives every digit its own token, so six hundred

198
00:10:31,558 --> 00:10:36,218
becomes three tokens: six, zero, zero. The reasoning is

199
00:10:36,218 --> 00:10:39,288
that numbers and mathematics are represented more clearly that

200
00:10:39,288 --> 00:10:39,731
way.

201
00:10:39,951 --> 00:10:43,871
One caution. This is not parsing. A tokenizer that

202
00:10:43,871 --> 00:10:47,761
handles indentation and keywords well makes the model's job

203
00:10:47,761 --> 00:10:50,421
easier. It does not make the code it generates

204
00:10:50,421 --> 00:10:51,271
correct.

205
00:10:52,010 --> 00:10:54,620
Why does any of this matter in practice? Because

206
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:57,180
a language model has a fixed number of positions

207
00:10:57,180 --> 00:10:59,420
it can look at. That limit is counted in

208
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:02,378
tokens, not in words, and not in characters.

209
00:11:02,652 --> 00:11:06,552
Picture a context of twelve positions. A finely split

210
00:11:06,552 --> 00:11:09,512
version of a sentence fills eleven of them. Only

211
00:11:09,512 --> 00:11:11,098
one position is left.

212
00:11:11,237 --> 00:11:15,925
The same sentence, tokenized with larger pieces, needs six.

213
00:11:15,925 --> 00:11:19,627
Same text, twice the room to spare. These particular

214
00:11:19,627 --> 00:11:21,927
counts are made up to make the point, but

215
00:11:21,927 --> 00:11:23,438
the effect is real.

216
00:11:23,664 --> 00:11:27,454
So the tokenizer decides how much text fits, and

217
00:11:27,454 --> 00:11:30,092
how much work the model does to read it.

218
00:11:30,092 --> 00:11:33,474
One caution though. A lower token count does not

219
00:11:33,474 --> 00:11:37,534
by itself make a model better. Architecture, training, and

220
00:11:37,534 --> 00:11:40,365
the context limit all matter just as much.

221
00:11:41,030 --> 00:11:44,180
Before we finish, a question. We ran the same

222
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:46,880
string through four trained tokenizers, and they did not

223
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:49,405
agree. Which statement does that support?

224
00:11:49,622 --> 00:11:54,632
One: every tokenizer produces the same sequence. Two: every

225
00:11:54,632 --> 00:12:00,002
character becomes exactly one token. Three: different trained tokenizers

226
00:12:00,002 --> 00:12:03,612
can segment the same input differently, and can preserve

227
00:12:03,612 --> 00:12:05,131
or lose parts of it.

228
00:12:05,356 --> 00:12:08,486
The answer is three. BERT lowercased the word before

229
00:12:08,486 --> 00:12:12,519
splitting it. GPT-2 recovered the emoji from raw bytes,

230
00:12:12,519 --> 00:12:16,226
and BERT could not. And GPT-4 used two pieces

231
00:12:16,226 --> 00:12:21,391
where FLAN-T5 used five. Same input, different outcomes, every time.

232
00:12:22,067 --> 00:12:24,897
So let us put the journey back together. Raw

233
00:12:24,897 --> 00:12:28,637
text is segmented into tokens. Each token is looked

234
00:12:28,637 --> 00:12:32,311
up in the vocabulary and becomes an integer ID.

235
00:12:32,311 --> 00:12:35,358
And that sequence of IDs is what the model receives.

236
00:12:35,636 --> 00:12:38,256
Those IDs now move inside the model, where a

237
00:12:38,256 --> 00:12:41,736
second table is waiting. Each ID selects one row

238
00:12:41,736 --> 00:12:43,966
of that table, and the row is a learned

239
00:12:43,966 --> 00:12:47,745
vector. That vector is what the model actually computes with.

240
00:12:49,299 --> 00:12:52,379
Keep the distinction clear. An ID is a discrete

241
00:12:52,379 --> 00:12:55,859
label. It has no size and no direction. The

242
00:12:55,859 --> 00:12:58,419
meaning lives in the vector it selects, not in

243
00:12:58,419 --> 00:12:59,685
the number itself.

244
00:12:59,973 --> 00:13:03,113
And one consequence that is easy to miss. A

245
00:13:03,113 --> 00:13:06,323
pretrained language model is tied to the tokenizer it

246
00:13:06,323 --> 00:13:08,993
was trained with. It cannot simply swap in a

247
00:13:08,993 --> 00:13:12,143
different one without being trained again. Which leaves our

248
00:13:12,143 --> 00:13:15,918
next question: how can a list of numbers represent meaning?
